// Offshore fault structures: "54海構造merge.json" (Chen & Shyu 2025 shapefiles) merged with the
// geometry workbook "mmc2.xlsx" by the layer's leading number. Paper: BSSA doi 10.1785/0120250009.
//
// Coordinates: the source geojson is EPSG:3826 (TWD97 TM2) metres despite declaring CRS84; every
// vertex here is the inverse-projected WGS84 lon/lat, sharing one frame with the land faults.
//
// DEPTH DATUM -- the workbook's "Depth 1"/"Depth 2" are the SEISMOGENIC DEPTH, i.e. the vertical
// distance measured DOWN FROM THE SEABED, not below sea level. The paper states this, and the
// workbook's arithmetic confirms it: Width == Depth1 / sin(dip) holds exactly for every plain
// single-ramp structure, whereas (Depth1 - Depth0) / sin(dip) does not. So segments[].depth is the
// workbook value verbatim -- already trace-relative, with nothing subtracted.
//
// `seabedKm` is Depth 0, the average water depth along the fault line. The trace sits there rather
// than at elevation 0, and it is one value per structure, so a trace is level rather than following
// the seabed -- the DEM carries no bathymetry.
//
// SEGMENT KINDS -- {depth, dip} is a dipping ramp: the row advances downdip by
// (depth delta)/tan(dip). {depth, horizKm} is a FLAT: the depth is unchanged and the row advances
// horizontally by horizKm, which is how the workbook's "Horizental length (km)" column is drawn.
// Structures 29-48 are ramp-flat thrusts whose flats lie on the decollement beneath domain E; the
// paper notes structures 29 to 49 may rupture together along it (Table 2, "Detachment fault").
//
// `widthTableKm` vs `widthFromSegmentsKm` -- the workbook's Width against the width implied by the
// segment stack. They agree to 3 decimals for 50 of the 55 structures. Where they differ (22, 27,
// 51, 54A, 54B) the workbook's Width equals Area/Length exactly, meaning it was carried over from a
// published subsurface model rather than computed from these Depth/Dip cells; the geometry here
// follows Depth/Dip, which is what defines a 3D surface.
//
// Values used: the MEAN of each min/mean/max triple.
//
// Rows whose ID ends in "-2" are multifault rupture scenarios, not structures -- per Table 1 of the
// paper, "Structure numbers suffixed by -2 represent calculated results for parameters where the
// structure ruptures simultaneously with other structures" -- so they are excluded. Rows 22b and 23b
// (workbook note "dip change") are alternative ramp-flat readings of 22 and 23 and are also excluded.
//
// `parts`: one polyline per strand. A structure mapped as several disjoint strands keeps them
// separate rather than joining them with invented segments; all strands share its dip and depths.
//
// ids are offset by 1000 so they never collide with the 48 land faults (which own 1..48). 54A and 54B
// have no plain "54" structure, so they take 1054 and 1055.

